#
# A SEED FOR basenet.conf.local - copy it, do not read it and retype it.
#
#     cp basenet.conf.local.example basenet.conf.local
#
# basenet.conf holds the defaults and is not yours to edit. Anything you set
# here is sourced after it and wins. `.gitignore` names basenet.conf.local, and
# the publication tooling excludes it, so your real values cannot be committed
# or exported by accident. This .example file has no real values in it and is
# published on purpose.
#
# EVERY ADDRESS BELOW IS FROM 192.0.2.0/24, which RFC 5737 reserves for
# documentation. None of them can be anybody's machine, so nothing here works
# until you replace them - which is the point: a plausible-looking address that
# half works is worse than one that obviously does not.
#
# You need three things right before anything installs. The rest have working
# defaults.

# ---- 1. WHERE THE CLUSTER REACHES YOUR IDENTITY PROVIDER --------------------
#
# THE ONE SETTING PEOPLE GET WRONG. This is the address a POD uses, not the
# address you use. Inside a pod, localhost is that pod, so 127.0.0.1 and
# localhost are both wrong here even when the provider runs on your own
# machine. Use the address your workstation has on the network the cluster can
# see - `ip -4 addr` will show it - and the port the provider listens on.
#
# The bundled provider pins its issuer to whatever you put here, and that one
# string has to be reachable identically from the cluster and from your
# browser, because the discovery document, every endpoint URL and the `iss` of
# every token are compared literally.
STR_OIDC_BASE_URL=http://192.0.2.10:32002

# ---- 2. THE STORAGE CLASS ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Every database asks for a PersistentVolumeClaim. `kubectl get storageclass`
# names what your cluster has. k3s ships local-path; kubeadm ships nothing at
# all until you install a provisioner, and a stack that hangs with every
# PostgreSQL pod Pending is this setting.
STR_STORAGE_CLASS=local-path

# ---- 3. WHO CAN SIGN IN -----------------------------------------------------
#
# name:password pairs for the bundled provider's login page, comma separated,
# plain text. One per person who will open a wallet. The name becomes the `sub`
# of every token that person gets, so it has to match STR_MO_WALLET_USER for
# the MemberOrg whose wallet they open - the default pattern is <name>-user.
#
# A replacement provider keeps its own users and ignores this line.
STR_JWTMINT_USERS=sv-user:change-me,memberorg-a-user:change-me

# ---- The rest have defaults that work ---------------------------------------

# The Splice release. Bump it, then run ./shared/render.sh BEFORE installing
# anything: the required set of values changes between releases, and what it
# rejects is the change list.
#STR_SPLICE_VERSION=0.7.4

# The audience the network-side apps require on an inbound token. Any string,
# as long as your provider mints it and both sides agree.
#STR_OIDC_AUDIENCE=https://sv.example.com

# The audience a MemberOrg's participant requires. Same rule, different side.
#STR_MO_LEDGER_AUDIENCE=https://ledger_api.example.com

# ---- Pointing at your own registry ------------------------------------------
#
# Blank pulls from the vendor. Set this and every image reference is rewritten
# to your mirror. ./shared/images.sh prints the list to mirror, and
# ./shared/download_images.sh pulls them by digest and writes SHA256SUMS.
#
# THE LAYOUT MATTERS - read docs/images.md before setting it. The charts append
# their own image names to this value, so your registry has to carry them at
# <STR_IMAGE_REPO>/<the vendor's image name>:<tag>. A registry that holds them
# somewhere else needs the per-image overrides that document describes.
#STR_IMAGE_REPO=192.0.2.20:5000/basenet

# PostgreSQL is the exception: its chart renders the WHOLE reference, so this
# value carries the registry itself when STR_IMAGE_REPO is set.
#STR_PG_IMAGE=postgres:14

# ---- A MemberOrg against a real identity provider ---------------------------
#
# The defaults in basenet.conf suit the bundled provider. Against Keycloak or
# anything else, these are the keys that move - and nothing else does.
#
# Any STR_MO_* key is overridden for ONE MemberOrg by appending its name in
# capitals with dashes as underscores, so a second organization with different
# credentials needs no second file:
#
#     STR_MO_CLIENT_SECRET_MEMBERORG_A=<that client's real secret>
#     STR_MO_WALLET_USER_MEMBERORG_A=<that person's sub at your provider>
#
# %s in a value is the MemberOrg's name.
#
#STR_OIDC_WELL_KNOWN_URL=https://192.0.2.30/realms/basenet/.well-known/openid-configuration
#STR_OIDC_JWKS_URL=https://192.0.2.30/realms/basenet/protocol/openid-connect/certs
#STR_MO_CLIENT_ID=%s-validator
#STR_MO_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me
# Keycloak's sub is the service account's id, not the client id.
#STR_MO_LEDGER_USER=service-account-%s-validator
#STR_MO_WALLET_USER=%s-user

# ---- A MemberOrg on another cluster -----------------------------------------
#
# Blank means this BaseNet Validator, reached from another namespace on the
# same cluster. A MemberOrg somewhere else needs addresses that cluster can
# reach - and if any MemberOrg will ever be off-cluster, read the warning about
# domain.sequencerPublicUrl in README_LONG_TIME.md BEFORE the first install.
# It is advertised when the network is founded and cannot be corrected after.
#
#STR_EXT_SCAN_URL=http://192.0.2.40:5012
#STR_EXT_SPONSOR_URL=http://192.0.2.40:5014
#STR_EXT_SEQUENCER_URL=http://192.0.2.40:5008
